(cl-text

; ============================================================
; エルブラン空間における命題の不在と四句分別
; Herbrand Universe, Absence of Propositions, and Catuṣkoṭi
; Chiba Masaomi (千葉 将臣)
; 2026-02-19
; ============================================================

(comment "
Abstract:
This text formalizes the logical necessity of the fourfold negation 
(catuṣkoṭi: P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)) for demonstrating the complete 
absence of a proposition P in Herbrand space, distinct from 
unsatisfiability determination alone.
")

; ============================================================
; 1. CORE DEFINITIONS: HERBRAND SPACE STRUCTURE
; ============================================================

(define-sort HerbrandUniverse
  (documentation "The set of all ground terms generated from constants 
    and function symbols in the signature. Infinite structure: 
    H₀ = {a, f(a), f(f(a)), ...}"))

(define-sort HerbrandBase
  (documentation "The set of all ground atoms over the Herbrand universe."))

(define-sort Signature
  (documentation "Language signature containing function, constant, 
    and predicate symbols."))

(define-sort Language
  (documentation "A formal language L determined by a signature."))

(define-sort Proposition
  (documentation "A well-formed formula in the language."))

; ============================================================
; 2. REMOVAL TYPES: FOUR LEVELS OF MODIFICATION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 2: Premises and Terminology

Four distinct levels of modification to Herbrand space:
1. Signature Removal: removing function/constant/predicate symbols
2. Herbrand Universe Removal: restricting ground terms
3. Herbrand Base Removal: restricting ground atoms
4. Theory Removal: modifying axioms or inference rules

The 'removal' operation is the inverse of addition:
restricting the linguistic capacity to form expressions.")

(define-sort RemovalType)

(define SignatureRemoval : RemovalType
  (documentation "Removal of predicate, function, or constant symbols."))

(define UniverseRemoval : RemovalType
  (documentation "Restriction of the set of ground terms."))

(define BaseRemoval : RemovalType
  (documentation "Restriction of the set of ground atoms."))

(define TheoryRemoval : RemovalType
  (documentation "Modification of inference rules or axioms."))

; ============================================================
; 3. DISTINCTION: REMOVAL vs. UNSATISFIABILITY
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Provisional Definition of 'Removal':

Removal = A linguistic operation that deprives the signature 
(and thus the Herbrand base) of the ability to form certain 
well-formed formulas.

This differs fundamentally from making a formula false:
- Unsatisfiability: Formula remains generablen but constrained to be false
- Removal: Formula becomes ungenerabel; the very form cannot be expressed

In symbols:
- Unsatisfiability(P, T): P is generable in L, but T |≠ P (P is not derivable)
- Removal(P, L): P is not generable in L (P cannot be said at all)")

; A proposition is generable in a language
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (Generable P L)
    (exists ((wf))
      (and (WellFormed wf L)
          (= wf P)))))

; Removal as linguistic operation
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (L' Language))
  (if (and (RestrictedLanguage L' L)
          (Removes L' P))
    (and (not (Generable P L'))
        (forall ((i))
          (not (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L'))))))

; Key distinction
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (not (iff (Unsatisfiable P T)
           (CompleteRemoval P L))))

; ============================================================
; 4. CATUṢKOṬI: THE FOUR FORMS
; ============================================================

(comment "
The four forms of catuṣkoṭi that must be removed:

Form 1: P (Affirmation / Assertion)
  - The positive assertion of the proposition
  
Form 2: ¬P (Simple Negation)
  - The straightforward negation
  
Form 3: P∧¬P (Conjunction: Affirmation AND Negation)
  - The contradiction form
  
Form 4: ¬(P∨¬P) (Negation of the Law of Excluded Middle)
  - Denies that P or not-P must hold

Together, these four exhaust the logical space of positions a 
proposition might occupy.")

(define-sort CatuṣkoṭiForm)

; Define the four forms
(forall ((P Proposition))
  (and (= (Form1 P) P)
      (= (Form2 P) (not P))
      (= (Form3 P) (and P (not P)))
      (= (Form4 P) (not (or P (not P))))))

; The complete catuṣkoṭi set
(forall ((P Proposition))
  (= (CatuṣkoṭiSet P)
    (setof (Form1 P) (Form2 P) (Form3 P) (Form4 P))))

; Index over the four forms
(forall ((P Proposition) (i Integer))
  (if (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4))
    (exists ((form CatuṣkoṭiForm))
      (= form (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i)))))

; ============================================================
; 5. MAIN PROPOSITIONS: COMPLETE REMOVAL CONDITION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Proposition 1 (Complete Removal Condition for Absence):

In Herbrand space, to demonstrate the absence of proposition P 
in a manner other than unsatisfiability determination alone, 
one must remove all four forms from the language:
  P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)

If any one form remains in the language, inference can still 
extract information about P.")

; Complete removal of all four forms
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)
    (and (not (Generable (Form1 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form2 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form3 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form4 P) L)))))

; If any form remains, information remains extractable
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (exists ((i Integer))
        (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4)
            (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L)))
    (exists ((info))
      (and (InformationAbout P info)
          (Extractable info L)))))

; Contrapositive: complete removal implies no information
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)
    (forall ((info))
      (not (and (InformationAbout P info)
               (Extractable info L))))))

; Complete absence in Herbrand space
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)
    (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)))

; ============================================================
; 6. MAIN PROPOSITIONS: CATUṢKOṬI AS STRUCTURE
; ============================================================

(comment "
Proposition 2 (Interpretation as Catuṣkoṭi):

The four forms (P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)) constitute the 
catuṣkoṭi (Buddhist fourfold negation structure).

This is not merely a rhetorical choice but emerges naturally 
from the requirement of complete removal.")

; Catuṣkoṭi structure
(forall ((P Proposition))
  (iff (IsCatuṣkoṭiStructure P)
    (= (CatuṣkoṭiSet P)
      (setof (Form1 P) (Form2 P) (Form3 P) (Form4 P)))))

; The necessity of this structure
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (GoalIsCompleteAbsence P L)
    (Requires (CatuṣkoṭiStructure P))))

; ============================================================
; 7. SHORT-CIRCUITING: METHODOLOGICAL RISK
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 2 continued: Short-circuiting Risk

Short-circuit (短絡): Terminating verification upon obtaining 
unsatisfiability judgment and concluding P's 'absence' without 
completing the removal of all four forms.

This carries the epistemological risk of sliding into 'empty view' 
(śūnya-dṛṣṭi) - the Buddhist philosophical error of misconstruing 
emptiness as an external imposition rather than a structural property.

Methodological Stance:
We do not normatively prohibit unsatisfiability determination itself,
but rather make visible how short-circuiting connects to empty view,
thereby justifying the logical necessity of pursuing complete removal.")

; Define short-circuiting
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (iff (ShortCircuit P L T)
    (and (UnsatisfiabilityDetermined P T)
        (not (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)))))

; Risk of empty view
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (if (ShortCircuit P L T)
    (RisksEmptyView P L)))

; Temptation quantified by complexity imbalance
(comment "
Footnote: The 'temptation' of short-circuiting 
(pressure to conclude from minimal information) can be 
quantified by comparing Kolmogorov complexity:

If K(unsatisfiability-determination) << K(catuṣkoṭi-verification),
then short-circuit risk is high.

Concrete formalization is reserved for future work.")

(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (if (HighComplexityDisparity 
        (K (UnsatisfiabilityDetermined P T))
        (K (CompleteRemovalVerification P L)))
    (HighShortCircuitRisk P L T)))

; ============================================================
; 8. DISTINCTION: UNSATISFIABILITY vs. REMOVAL
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 4.1: Unsatisfiability Determination and Empty View

Unsatisfiability determination shows that under theory T, 
proposition P cannot hold. But this operation:
  - Leaves P's generability in the language intact
  - Applies external constraints (axioms, inference rules)
  - 'Crushes' P via external negation, not structural removal

Complete linguistic removal:
  - Operates at the signature/language level
  - Makes the very form inexpressible
  - Achieves the state: P 'cannot be said at all'
  - Prevents reification (re-objectification) of the proposition")

; Unsatisfiability under a theory
(forall ((P Proposition) (T Theory))
  (iff (Unsatisfiable P T)
    (forall ((model))
      (if (Model model T)
        (not (Satisfies model P))))))

; Unsatisfiability determination
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (iff (UnsatisfiabilityDetermined P T)
    (and (Generable P L)
        (Unsatisfiable P T))))

; Linguistic removal
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (LinguisticRemoval P L)
    (and (not (Generable P L))
        (forall ((i Integer))
          (if (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4))
            (not (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L)))))))

; The key distinction: different operations
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (if (and (UnsatisfiabilityDetermined P T)
          (not (LinguisticRemoval P L)))
    (and (ExternalizesEmptiness P T)
        (RisksEmptyView P L))))

; ============================================================
; 9. EMPTY VIEW ERROR (ŚŪNYA-DṚṢṬI)
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Buddhist philosophical concept 'empty view' (śūnya-dṛṣṭi) 
refers to the error of attributing emptiness as a property 
imparted 'from outside' to an object, rather than 
discovering emptiness as the structural nature of the object.

In logical terms:
  Empty view model: Language retains P's form, but external 
                    constraints make P non-obtainable
  Proper removal: Language itself removes the generability 
                  of all four forms
  
The unsatisfiability approach maps onto empty view:
  - P's form remains in the language
  - External theory 'crushes' it
  - Emptiness is externalized, not structural")

; Empty view error
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (T Theory))
  (iff (EmptyViewError P L T)
    (and (Generable P L)
        (ExternalConstraintMakesUnobtainable P T))))

; Proper structural removal
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (StructuralRemoval P L)
    (LinguisticRemoval P L)))

; Consequence of structural removal
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (StructuralRemoval P L)
    (StructuralEmptiness P L)))

; ============================================================
; 10. HIERARCHICAL STAGES OF REMOVAL
; ============================================================

(comment "
The four stages of removal must be pursued in order:

Stage 1: Removal of P (Affirmative form)
Stage 2: Removal of ¬P (Negation form)
Stage 3: Removal of P∧¬P (Contradiction form)
Stage 4: Removal of ¬(P∨¬P) (Negation of excluded middle)

At each stage, short-circuiting creates the risk of 
re-objectification (再対象化): the proposition settles 
back into a reified position.")

; Define stages
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (i Integer))
  (iff (StageCompleted P L i)
    (not (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L))))

; All stages must be completed
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (CompleteRemovalProcess P L)
    (forall ((i Integer))
      (if (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4))
        (StageCompleted P L i)))))

; Risk at each intermediate stage
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language) (i Integer))
  (if (and (StageCompleted P L i)
          (< i 4)
          (not (forall ((j Integer))
                (if (and (>= j 1) (<= j 4))
                  (StageCompleted P L j)))))
    (RisksReobjectification P L i)))

; ============================================================
; 11. UNIVERSAL QUANTIFICATION OVER INFINITE HERBRAND SPACE
; ============================================================

(comment "
The infinite nature of Herbrand space is essential:
H₀ = {a, f(a), f(f(a)), f(f(f(a))), ...}

The removal must apply uniformly to all instances 
generated in this infinite structure.")

; Infinite Herbrand universe
(define InfiniteHerbrandUniverse
  (forall ((n Integer))
    (exists ((term))
      (and (GroundTerm term)
          (= term (IterateFunction f a n))))))

; Universal removal over all instances
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (UniversalRemoval P L)
    (forall ((t))
      (if (GroundTerm t)
        (and (not (Generable (ApplyToTerm (Form1 P) t) L))
            (not (Generable (ApplyToTerm (Form2 P) t) L))
            (not (Generable (ApplyToTerm (Form3 P) t) L))
            (not (Generable (ApplyToTerm (Form4 P) t) L)))))))

; Completeness requires universal removal
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (UniversalRemoval P L)
    (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)))

; ============================================================
; 12. LOCAL vs. GLOBAL REMOVAL
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 4.2 Supplementary: Local Removal and Metalanguage Grounding

Important distinction:
- 'Saying something' is a meta-linguistic activity
- The removal of P's four forms is a LOCAL operation 
  on the object language
- Complete global removal would destroy the grounding 
  for all logical comparison

Local Removal: Remove P's four forms while preserving 
  the language's capacity to discuss removal itself.
  
Global Removal: Remove all vocabulary - leads to silence,
  destroying the logical basis for the very argument.

Key point: Local removal of P's four forms does NOT 
require destroying all language. It preserves the meta-
linguistic footing necessary to argue for the logical 
necessity of catuṣkoṭi.")

; Local removal
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (LocalRemoval P L)
    (and (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)
        (exists ((L' Language))
          (and (ProperSubLanguage L' L)
              (forall ((Q Proposition))
                (if (not (= Q P))
                  (forall ((i Integer))
                    (if (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4))
                      (iff (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi Q i) L)
                          (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi Q i) L')))))))))))

; Global removal
(forall ((L Language))
  (iff (GlobalRemoval L)
    (forall ((P Proposition))
      (forall ((i Integer))
        (if (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4))
          (not (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L)))))))

; Global removal leads to silence
(forall ((L Language))
  (if (GlobalRemoval L)
    (SilentLanguage L)))

; But Buddhist emptiness is NOT silence
(forall ()
  (not (iff (BuddhistEmptiness) (SilentLanguage))))

; Rather, prevent reification without destroying discourse
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (LocalRemoval P L)
    (and (PreventiveEmptiness P L)
        (CanStillDiscussRemoval))))

; ============================================================
; 13. METALANGUAGE AND THE PARADOX OF EMPTY REMOVAL
; ============================================================

(comment "
Paradox of Global Removal:

If we remove ALL vocabulary from the object language 
(global removal), then:
1. The statement 'nothing can be said' becomes 
   formally possible in the metalanguage
2. But the grounding for comparing which four forms 
   to remove is lost
3. The logical analysis of why catuṣkoṭi is necessary 
   becomes incoherent

Solution: We pursue LOCAL REMOVAL - removing P's four 
forms while preserving the language's capacity to discuss 
removal itself.

This avoids:
- Extreme emptying of vocabulary
- Destruction of logical comparison
- Making the argument self-defeating

The metalanguage can coherently state: 'In language L, 
the four forms of P are removed, allowing discussion of 
why this removal is logically necessary.'")

; Local removal preserves metalogical grounding
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (LocalRemoval P L)
    (and (not (GlobalRemoval L))
        (CanDiscussRemoval)
        (CanCompareLogicalStructures)
        (ArgumentCoherentAndNonCircular))))

; The risk of over-emptying
(forall ((L Language))
  (if (ExtremeEmptying L)
    (and (CanSayNothingMetatranslated)
        (LoseGroundingForComparison)
        (RendersArgumentAboutEmptinessIncoherent))))

; ============================================================
; 14. NECESSITY OF CATUṢKOṬI FRAMEWORK
; ============================================================

(comment "
Why is catuṣkoṭi necessary and natural for demonstrating absence?

Answer: It covers all four possible 'stands' or 'positions' 
that a proposition might occupy in the logical space:

1. Affirmative position: P is asserted
2. Negative position: P is negated
3. Paradoxical position: both P and ¬P coexist
4. Ultra-negative position: neither P nor ¬P applies

To prevent ANY form of reification (substantialization), 
one must systematically remove all four positions.

This is not merely rhetorical but formally necessary:
if any one form remains in the language, inference systems 
can still extract information about P and reify it into 
one of the positions.")

; Lemma: If any form remains, information remains
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (exists ((i Integer))
        (and (>= i 1) (<= i 4)
            (Generable (SelectCatuṣkoṭi P i) L)))
    (exists ((position))
      (and (LogicalPosition position)
          (CanBeReified P position L)))))

; Converse: complete removal prevents all reification
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L)
    (forall ((position))
      (not (CanBeReified P position L)))))

; ============================================================
; 15. SUMMARY: COMPLETE ABSENCE DEFINITION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Core Theorem:

To treat the absence of P in Herbrand space as a concept 
distinct from mere unsatisfiability, one must simultaneously 
remove all four forms:
  P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)

This condition is both necessary and sufficient for 
complete absence in Herbrand space.")

; Complete absence in Herbrand space
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)
    (and (not (Generable (Form1 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form2 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form3 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form4 P) L)))))

; This is the minimum requirement for 'absence'
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (MinimumRequirementForAbsence P L)
    (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)))

; ============================================================
; 16. RELATION TO "ALL IS EMPTY" (SABBAṀ SUÑÑAṀ)
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 4.2: 'All is Empty' and Catuṣkoṭi Extension

Buddhist doctrine: 'All phenomena are empty' (sabbaṁ suññaṁ).

If we extend our framework universally:
  For every proposition P, remove all four forms from 
  Herbrand space.

This leads to apparent global removal - but with a crucial 
methodological understanding:

Buddhist emptiness is NOT mere silence or nihilism.
Rather, it is a method for deconstructing attachment 
and preventing reification.

Proper understanding:
The removal of all four forms for every P prevents the 
system from 'settling' into any reified position, while 
the metalanguage remains available for discussing why 
this systematic non-settlement is logically necessary.")

; Universal extension
(define UniversalCatuṣkoṭiRemoval
  (lambda (L)
    (forall ((P Proposition))
      (CompleteRemovalAllFourForms P L))))

; But this does NOT mean silence
(forall ((L Language))
  (if (UniversalCatuṣkoṭiRemoval L)
    (not (SilentLanguage L))))

; Rather, it means systematic non-reification
(forall ((L Language))
  (if (UniversalCatuṣkoṭiRemoval L)
    (PreventiveEmptinessUniversal L)))

; Buddhist emptiness is methodological
(forall ()
  (iff (BuddhistEmptiness)
    (and (SystematicRemovalOfAllFourForms)
        (DeconstructionOfAttachment)
        (PreventionOfReification)
        (MetalanguageStillAvailable))))

; ============================================================
; 17. FUTURE RESEARCH DIRECTIONS
; ============================================================

(comment "
Future Research Tasks (Note):

1. Relation to Herbrand Completeness Theorem:
   This framework operates outside classical Herbrand 
   completeness. Future work should clarify the formal 
   relationship between:
   - Classical Herbrand space (with completeness guarantee)
   - 'Looser' space emphasizing term generation structure

2. Meta-role of Term Generation:
   The recursive generation H₀ = {a, f(a), f(f(a)), ...}
   plays an essential role in catuṣkoṭi enumeration.
   Future work should make this explicit.

3. Kolmogorov Complexity Formalization:
   Using K(·), develop formal criteria for when 
   short-circuiting creates high risk.

4. Extension to Other Logical Systems:
   How does catuṣkoṭi necessity change under:
   - Intuitionistic logic
   - Linear logic
   - Paraconsistent logic

5. Two-Truths as Category Theory:
   Explore conventional truth (samvṛti-satya) / 
   ultimate truth (paramārtha-satya) distinction 
   via sliced categories.")

; ============================================================
; 18. CONCLUSION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Section 5: Conclusion

To treat the absence of P in Herbrand space as a concept 
distinct from mere unsatisfiability, one must simultaneously 
remove all four forms:
  P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)

This condition is both necessary and sufficient.

This perspective:
1. Provides the formal minimum requirement for discussing 'absence'
2. Justifies the Buddhist use of catuṣkoṭi on logical grounds
3. Prevents the error of empty view (śūnya-dṛṣṭi)
4. Frames emptiness as structural, not externally imposed

The catuṣkoṭi structure emerges naturally from the requirement 
of complete removal, making it not a rhetorical choice but 
a formal logical necessity.")

; Core final theorem
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)
    (and (not (Generable (Form1 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form2 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form3 P) L))
        (not (Generable (Form4 P) L)))))

; This is the minimum requirement
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (iff (MinimumRequirementForLogicalAbsence P L)
    (CompleteAbsenceInHerbrand P L)))

; The catuṣkoṭi structure is necessary, not merely rhetorical
(forall ((P Proposition) (L Language))
  (if (GoalIsCompleteAbsenceWithoutEmptyView P L)
    (CatuṣkoṭiFrameworkNecessary P L)))

; ============================================================
; END OF COMMON LOGIC FORMALIZATION
; ============================================================

) ; end cl-text
